# Twenty to thirty-one, read off digit cards, and how old you are.
lesson language-how-old v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=1 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[phrase.how-old-are-you]]"
  goal "Count from twenty to thirty-one in [[language.name]] and say how old you are."

  look {
    scene 37x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row party space=1 align=end {
          cake c candles=8 slices=0
          comic talk who=["boy", "girl"] says=["[[phrase.how-old-are-you]]", "[[phrase.i-am-eight-years-old]]"] moods=["happy", "excited"] with=["", ""] thinks=[0, 0] holds=["", ""] labels=["1", "2"]
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      To ask someone's age, say [[phrase.how-old-are-you]] The answer gives the number:
      [[phrase.i-am-eight-years-old]], I am eight. After [[num.twenty]] the numbers go on:
      [[num.twenty-one]], [[num.twenty-two]], [[num.twenty-three]], and on to [[num.thirty]] and
      [[num.thirty-one]].
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.how-old-name k=4
    }
    show language.how-old-name k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=6
    show language.how-old-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.how-old-write k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.how-old-name k=5 easy-k=2 hard-k=7
    show language.how-old-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.how-old-write k=6 easy-k=3
    show language.how-old-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.how-old-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.how-old-write k=4
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-how-old-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.how-old-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.how-old-are-you]] [[phrase.i-am-eight-years-old]] After [[num.twenty]]: [[num.twenty-one]], [[num.twenty-two]], and on to [[num.thirty-one]]."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is saying an age word for word the English way, which the two-star
    question is about, and a number in the twenties read as the teen it sounds like. The digit cards
    carry the number, so the child's job is only the word. The listening question is a dictation:
    read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or let the device play
    it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts there, while the words typed on their
    own in this lesson are taken without their accents. If the child is stuck, build twenty to
    thirty-one with two ten frames and one more, saying each. Off the page, ask three people how old
    they are and write their answers in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages
    programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "engage in conversations; ask and answer
    questions"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language activities: "Activities to ask and answer
    simple questions about each other's likes and wants, etc."; and CEFR A1, "Can ask and answer
    questions about personal details". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[num.twenty-eight]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[num.twenty-one]] and [[num.twenty-five]] and the words to write are [[num.thirty]] and [[num.twenty-two]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[num.thirty-one]] and [[num.twenty-four]] and the words to write are [[num.twenty-three]], [[num.twenty-seven]] and [[num.twenty-eight]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
